---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="NPC data system"
  description="How the game stores NPC identities, personalities, and visual data — from the master database to face textures."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'How the Game Works', href: `${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/` },
    { label: 'NPC data system' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>Speaker ID ranges</h2>
  <p>
    The dialog system identifies every speaker by a numeric ID. The ID
    determines whether the game renders a full 3D character model or
    displays text without a portrait.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Range</th><th>Type</th><th>Count</th><th>Rendering</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1000--1251</td><td>Named villager NPCs</td><td>252</td><td>Full 3D scene (face mesh + texture + body)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000+</td><td>System speakers</td><td>~42 in DB</td><td>No portrait (narrator, crowd, announcements)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>NPC data files</h2>
  <p>
    All NPC data lives under <code>data/npc/</code> (29 files total).
    The files below are the ones most relevant to modding and translation.
  </p>

  <h3>NpcData.lzs</h3>
  <p>
    The master NPC database. LZS-compressed, contains NPC names as binary
    strings. This is where the translation patch writes English NPC names.
    The ROM ships with Japanese names; the patch overwrites them in the
    decompressed binary.
  </p>

  <h3>NpcDataOfs.ofs</h3>
  <p>
    252 SZL-packed personality records, one per named NPC (IDs 1000--1251).
    Each record decompresses to 88--200 bytes.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Offset</th><th>Type</th><th>Field</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>+0x00</code></td><td>u8</td><td>constant <code>0x00</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x01</code></td><td>u8</td><td><code>personality_template</code> (0--10, 11 distinct types)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x02</code></td><td>u16</td><td><code>face_or_age</code> (0--30, 31 distinct values; not a direct index into the face texture file)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x04</code>--<code>+0x07</code></td><td>4 x u8</td><td>body parameters (height, build, etc.)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x08</code>--<code>+0x0b</code></td><td>4 x u8</td><td>clothing-layer IDs</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x0c</code> onward</td><td>UTF-16 LE</td><td>JP NPC name (null-terminated)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>after null</td><td>UTF-16 LE</td><td>greeting / quote</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Other NPC files</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>NpcCstm.ofs</code></td><td>140 customization parameter sets (1:1 with face textures). Controls per-NPC visual variations.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>NpcFas.npfs</code></td><td>NPC fashion / clothing data.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>NpcClothInfo.nci</code></td><td>NPC clothing info table.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>NpcSchedule.init</code></td><td>NPC daily schedule data.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Personality types</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>personality_template</code> byte at offset <code>+0x01</code>
    in each NpcDataOfs record determines an NPC's behavioral archetype.
    There are 11 distinct personality types (values 0--10). The personality
    type controls:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Which dialog files the NPC draws from (<code>msg20</code>--<code>msg31</code> each correspond to a personality grouping).</li>
    <li>Whether the NPC's dialog renders as a bubble or a window (the personality type feeds into the <code>bubble_sel</code> selector in the dialog renderer).</li>
    <li>Talk topic preferences and conversation style.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The personality system is what gives each NPC their characteristic way
    of speaking, even though many NPCs share the same underlying dialog
    templates.
  </p>

  <h2>Face textures and visual identity</h2>
  <p>
    Named NPCs are rendered in full 3D. There are no 2D portrait sprites.
    The face rendering pipeline uses three texture files:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>model/npc/tex/face/tfnpc.ofs</code></td><td>140 face textures (base face meshes). 252 NPCs share 140 faces; individual identity comes from body parameters, clothing layers, and hair.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>model/npc/tex/face/tfnpccmn.ofs</code></td><td>33 common face overlay textures (eyes, mouth positions, blush states). Composited at runtime to create expressions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>model/npc/tex/face/tfnpcguest.ofs</code></td><td>13 guest NPC face textures for special or temporary characters.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The <code>NpcCstm.ofs</code> customization records (140 entries, 1:1 with
    <code>tfnpc.ofs</code>) hold the per-NPC parameters that differentiate
    NPCs who share the same base face.
  </p>

  <h2>NPC stamps</h2>
  <p>
    <code>data/npcstamp/npcstamp.ofs</code> contains 140 SZL-packed RGCN
    entries. Despite the name, these are not NPC portrait images. They are
    small personality stamp icons used on the rumor board and trend ranking
    system. The shared palette and cell layout come from the sibling file
    <code>npcstpcmn.lzs</code>, an RESC container with NCER at offset
    <code>0xa0</code> and NCLR at offset <code>0x110</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Special character paths</h2>
  <p>
    Not all characters go through the villager personality system. Special
    characters like the principal, teachers, and shopkeepers use separate
    dialog paths that bypass the personality-based bubble/window selector.
    Their dialog routing is hardcoded in the ARM9 binary rather than driven
    by the <code>NpcDataOfs</code> <code>personality_template</code> byte.
  </p>

  <h2>NPC names in the translation</h2>
  <p>
    The translation patch updates NPC names in two places:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>NpcData.lzs</strong> -- the master binary that the game reads at runtime for display names.</li>
    <li><strong>The <code>npc_names</code> table in the translation database</strong> -- the source of truth for canonical English names, which feeds the NpcData.lzs binary writer during ROM rebuild.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    82 rows still have drift between the database canonical name and the
    binary. This is tracked internally but has not been fully reconciled.
  </p>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>data/npc/</code> -- 29 NPC data files in the ROM filesystem</li>
    <li><code>data/npcstamp/</code> -- stamp icon container</li>
    <li><code>model/npc/tex/face/</code> -- face texture containers</li>
    <li>NpcDataOfs record layout from binary analysis of decompressed SZL records</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
